/home/node/work/normal_work/HFT_Trading_Practice_Repo/docs/learning/vocab/0004-itch-order-book-latency.html

Use the dark-theme style (same CSS variables: --bg:#0f1117, --surface:#1a1d27, --surface2:#22263a, --border:#2e3450, --accent:#5b8dee, --accent2:#a78bfa, --green:#34d399, --yellow:#fbbf24, --red:#f87171, --text:#e2e8f0, --muted:#94a3b8, --code-bg:#0d1117, --inline-code-bg:#1e2436). Inline all CSS. max-width 860px container.

Title: "Vocab 0004 — ITCH 5.0, Order Book Reconstruction, and Latency Measurement"

Include a small label "VOCAB 0004" then h1, subtitle.

Include a search/filter input at the top that dynamically filters terms by name or definition text as the user types (pure JS, no libraries).

For each term, render a card with: term name (prominent), type badge (Protocol / Data Structure / Concept / Measurement / Hardware), one-line definition (bold), then a detailed explanation section, and a "From the codebase" code snippet where applicable.

Terms to include (12 total):

1. ITCH 5.0
Type: Protocol
One-line: Nasdaq TotalView-ITCH 5.0 — a binary, unidirectional market data protocol broadcasting every order event for US equities.
Detail: ITCH is a feed-only protocol (no requests). Each message is a fixed-length binary record. Message types include Add Order, Execute Order, Cancel Order, Delete Order, and Replace Order. All numeric fields are big-endian. The full spec is ~40 pages; this repo implements the 4 types needed for L2 book reconstruction.
Code: (none needed)

2. MoldUDP64
Type: Protocol
One-line: Nasdaq's UDP framing protocol that wraps ITCH messages: each message is preceded by a 2-byte big-endian length field.
Detail: MoldUDP64 frames messages for reliable multicast delivery. In Nasdaq's sample binary files the framing is simplified to just a 2-byte length prefix per message. The parser reads be16(buf+pos) to get msg_len, skips 2 bytes for the length, then processes msg_len bytes of payload.
Code:
```cpp
// From itch_parser.cpp parse_buffer():
while (pos + 2 <= len) {
    const uint16_t msg_len = be16(buf + pos);
    pos += 2;
    // ... process msg_len bytes at buf+pos
    pos += msg_len;
}
```

3. Order reference number
Type: Concept
One-line: A uint64_t unique identifier assigned by Nasdaq to each resting limit order; the primary key for all book operations.
Detail: Every Add Order message introduces a new order_ref. All subsequent Execute, Delete, and Replace messages reference an existing order_ref. The OrderBook stores orders in an unordered_map<uint64_t, OrderRecord> keyed by order_ref, enabling O(1) lookup when any subsequent message arrives.
Code:
```cpp
// From order_book.hpp:
std::unordered_map<uint64_t, OrderRecord> orders_;
// orders_[ref] → OrderRecord { side, price_raw, shares }
```

4. Price level
Type: Concept
One-line: An aggregated entry in the order book at a specific price; stores the total shares resting at that price across all orders.
Detail: Multiple orders can sit at the same price. The book maintains a single aggregate share count per price level. When an order is added at a price already in the map, its shares are added to the existing count. When shares drop to zero the price level entry is erased.
Code:
```cpp
// From order_book.hpp:
struct PriceLevel {
    uint32_t price_raw;   // fixed-point (divide by 10000 for real price)
    uint32_t shares;
};
```

5. L2 book (Level 2 order book)
Type: Concept
One-line: An order book that shows aggregate share volume at each price level but does not reveal individual order identities.
Detail: L2 data gives you bid/ask depth — how many shares are queued at each price. It does not tell you which participant owns which order. The baseline OrderBook in this repo reconstructs the full L2 depth from ITCH messages. The flat-array variant (OrderBookFlat) targets lower latency by replacing std::map with a fixed-size sorted array for the top N levels.
Code: (none needed)

6. Top-of-book
Type: Concept
One-line: The single best bid price and best ask price currently resting in the book — the innermost quotes.
Detail: Best bid = highest price a buyer will pay. Best ask = lowest price a seller will accept. The spread = ask - bid. In the codebase, top_of_book() returns optional<PriceLevel> for each side. The bids_ map (sorted descending) makes best_bid = bids_.begin(); the asks_ map (sorted ascending) makes best_ask = asks_.begin().
Code:
```cpp
// From order_book.hpp:
struct TopOfBook {
    std::optional<PriceLevel> best_bid;
    std::optional<PriceLevel> best_ask;
};
TopOfBook top_of_book() const;
```

7. Tick-to-trade latency
Type: Measurement
One-line: The elapsed time from when a market data message arrives to when the trading system emits a signal (or sends an order).
Detail: This is the latency budget for a market-making or momentum strategy. In bench_main.cpp, it is measured as the time to execute book.add() + make_signal(book). The parse step is not included — it occurs before the callback fires. In a real production system the start of the window would be the NIC hardware timestamp when the first byte of the UDP frame arrives.
Code:
```cpp
// From bench_main.cpp:
const uint64_t t0 = now_ns();
book.add(m.order_ref, m.side, m.price, m.shares);
itch::make_signal(book);
const uint64_t t1 = now_ns();
hist.record(t1 - t0);
```

8. CLOCK_MONOTONIC
Type: Hardware / OS
One-line: A POSIX clock that always increases — never jumps backward — making it correct for measuring elapsed time.
Detail: CLOCK_REALTIME represents wall-clock time and can be adjusted by the NTP daemon (forward or backward). CLOCK_MONOTONIC is guaranteed to be non-decreasing. On Linux, resolution is typically 1 ns with CONFIG_HZ_1000. CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW is immune even to NTP frequency adjustments but is Linux-only.
Code:
```cpp
// From bench_main.cpp:
static inline uint64_t now_ns() {
    struct timespec ts;
    clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &ts);
    return static_cast<uint64_t>(ts.tv_sec) * 1'000'000'000ULL
         + static_cast<uint64_t>(ts.tv_nsec);
}
```

9. HdrHistogram (High Dynamic Range Histogram)
Type: Data Structure
One-line: A histogram that uses power-of-2 buckets with sub-bucket resolution to record latency values across a wide range (1 ns – 68 s) with ~1% precision.
Detail: Unlike a fixed-bucket histogram, HDR never overflows for large values and never wastes resolution on small values. The latency::Histogram in this repo is a self-contained reimplementation (no external library). It uses the same percentile() API as the real HdrHistogram_c library. Production systems use the C or Java version which also supports lock-free concurrent recording.
Code:
```cpp
// From latency_histogram.hpp:
latency::Histogram hist(3);  // 3 significant figures
hist.record(t1 - t0);        // record one sample in ns
hist.percentile(50.0);       // p50
hist.percentile(99.0);       // p99
hist.percentile(99.9);       // p99.9
```

10. p50 / p99 / p99.9
Type: Measurement
One-line: Percentile latency values: p50 is the median; p99 means 99% of events are faster; p99.9 means 999 in 1000 are faster.
Detail: p50 tells you the typical case. p99 is the "tail budget" — your strategy must complete profitably even here. p99.9 catches rare spikes caused by OS scheduling jitter, GC pauses (in JVM-based systems), hash map rehashes, or TLB misses. Reporting mean latency alone is misleading because rare high-latency events dominate P&L in HFT without moving the mean much.
Code: (none needed)

11. Big-endian
Type: Concept
One-line: Byte order where the most significant byte is stored first (lowest address); the opposite of little-endian (x86 native order).
Detail: ITCH 5.0 specifies all multi-byte fields as big-endian (network byte order). x86 CPUs are natively little-endian, so every field must be byte-swapped before use. The be16/be32/be64 helpers in itch_parser.cpp do this manually using bit shifts — avoiding the overhead of htonl/ntohl or __builtin_bswap32 across compilers.
Code:
```cpp
// From itch_parser.cpp:
static inline uint32_t be32(const uint8_t* p) {
    return (static_cast<uint32_t>(p[0]) << 24 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[1]) << 16 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[2]) <<  8 |
            static_cast<uint32_t>(p[3]));
}
```

12. Fixed-point price
Type: Concept
One-line: An integer representation of price where the value is implicitly scaled by a fixed factor; ITCH 5.0 uses 4 decimal places (divide by 10,000).
Detail: Using integers for price eliminates floating-point rounding errors in comparisons and hash keys. $150.25 is stored as 1502500 (uint32_t). The price_raw field in OrderRecord and PriceLevel stores this raw value. Conversion to double (for display or signal emission) happens only at the output boundary: price_raw / 10000.0.
Code:
```cpp
// From signal.hpp:
sig.bid_price = tob.best_bid->price_raw / 10000.0;
sig.ask_price = tob.best_ask->price_raw / 10000.0;
sig.mid       = (sig.bid_price + sig.ask_price) / 2.0;
```

Style: each term card has a visible bottom border, the term name is large, the type badge is a small pill colored by type (Protocol=accent, Data Structure=accent2, Concept=yellow, Measurement=green, Hardware/OS=red). The search input filters cards in real-time. Include a count showing "Showing N of 12 terms".

The file should be completely self-contained HTML with inline JS and CSS. No external dependencies. Write it now.
